package helios:system@0.1.0;

/// How a program names a part of its own linear memory to the kernel.
///
/// Everything a program shares with hardware or with another program
/// without a copy — a frame buffer, a window's pixels, a command buffer,
/// a device's registers — appears inside memory the program already
/// has, at a place the program chose. The program allocates whole pages
/// from its own allocator and passes them as a `span`; the kernel
/// changes what backs those pages and nothing else about them. They
/// stay inside the memory's current size, so every access to them,
/// scalar or bulk, is bounded exactly as an access to any other byte
/// the program owns.
///
/// When whatever was shown there ends — the resource is dropped, the
/// grant is released — the pages are ordinary zeroed memory again and
/// the program may free them. Freeing them while they are still shown
/// hands a device's memory to the allocator; `helios-api` holds the
/// two together so that cannot be written.
interface memory {
  /// A run of whole wasm pages of the calling instance's linear memory.
  ///
  /// `offset` and `length` are both multiples of 65536, `length` is not
  /// zero, the run ends at or below the memory's current size, and it
  /// overlaps nothing the kernel is already showing in this memory. A
  /// span that breaks any of these is refused with the interface's
  /// `bad-span`.
  ///
  /// The call a span is passed to says how long it has to be: the size
  /// of what is shown, rounded up to whole wasm pages. Where what is
  /// shown ends before the span does, the rest of the span stays the
  /// program's own memory.
  record span {
    /// Byte offset of the first page.
    offset: u64,
    /// How many bytes the run covers.
    length: u64,
  }
}
